{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head cd086606e2ce01cb1efbfa3f671b1428fe2c5784, tree 75711e422890f7d68203be1ef9d265fd410ce012, on builder/cb2-controls-20261116, all 7 artifacts, for request a7f16135 under my promise c2d89712. This verdict names the one implementation: CB-2 (request ced2f264, promise 340635c1, report 5653ae0d, the CB-2 note, primary). Main e3392a05 is the base.\n\nThe gesture binding is sound. My one required change is what happens when the reconciler refuses a tree.\n\n## Finding: a refused plan freezes the card on its old content\n\n`plan()` throws when the next tree changes the relative order of matched siblings (reconcile.ts:151). It also throws when a retained surface would move, or when the next tree holds a live node. The note presents the throw as safe: \"the live tree stays as it was\". But `render()` does not catch it. By then `accept()` has already replaced `display`. So the screen keeps the previous card's text, values and buttons, while `display` holds the new card.\n\nEvery later render for that card identity rebuilds the same order and throws again. Nothing on the screen changes until the identity changes. That includes the CHANGED_GESTURE notice, `showError` messages and the busy state, because each of them reaches the screen only through `render()`. A press on the stale card fails `currentGesture` and calls `render()`, which throws again. So the member sees an old card that does nothing when pressed.\n\nNo act signs against the wrong card, because `act()` checks the gesture and sends the captured cut. The failure is a stuck, wrong display. Before CB-2 that could not happen.\n\nThe throw needs only a refresh within one card identity that swaps two matched siblings under one parent. `cardIdentity()` leaves out the revision and the cut. So the keyed lists whose order comes from data can do it: actions (`action:\u003cid\u003e` in `actions:\u003cidentity\u003e`), related links (`link:…`), reader buttons, log rows (`log:…`) and entries (`entry:…`). So can unkeyed siblings of different tags or classes that change places. Nothing in `bridge.test.ts` or the witness covers a reorder.\n\n## What I ask\n\n1. When a plan throws, do not leave the old tree up. Fall back to a full rebuild of the card from the desired tree. Retire the old controls (`retireControls`), and accept that a press in flight is lost, as it was before CB-2. Keep the surface host under its placeholder rule. If the fallback cannot keep the host in its parent, show a notice rather than freeze.\n2. Add a `bridge.test.ts` case. It renders, then refreshes the same identity with two keyed actions in swapped order. It asserts that the new order and labels appear, and that a press on a swapped button sends nothing (CHANGED_GESTURE) or runs the action it shows.\n3. In the note, replace \"the live tree stays as it was\" with what the fallback does.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- `reconcile.ts`:\n  - A live child matches a wanted child by key, or by tag and `class` when neither has a key.\n  - `attributes()` copies `disabled` and `open`.\n  - `fresh()` refuses a live node.\n  - A retained surface is placed through its `Comment` placeholder and never reparented.\n  - `retire()` clears control state on removal, and `close()` retires the root.\n- `bindControl` and `updateControlBinding`:\n  - A pointer press captures at `pointerdown`.\n  - A key press captures at the first `keydown`, and repeats do not capture again.\n  - A blur or a window blur cancels a key gesture, so a later assistive click uses the current binding.\n  - A click on a detached or disabled element clears its state.\n  - `pointerup` outside the element and `pointercancel` drop the press.\n- `main.ts`. `captureGesture` takes view, cut, address, identity and position. Each bound action checks `currentGesture` before it sends. `act()` sends `gesture.view` and `gesture.cut`, so a press acts on the cut the member saw, and the server refuses a stale cut. `handoff()` words the chat message from the captured identity.\n- Security: no finding. The reconciler copies attributes and text from trees that `main.ts` builds, never from HTML strings. No new message, tool call or permission was added, and a changed card sends nothing.\n- Native coverage. The 16 cases are the scale tier, which `scale-selection.json` omits because none of the 7 changed paths matches a scale path. GS-1 set that rule, and none of the 16 imports `packages/mcp/browser`. I accept the selection.\n- Scope. The 7 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only e3392a05 cd086606`.\n- The gate, END 918ae27d. `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-16-cb2-gate/gate.log` names head cd086606 and tree 75711e42 with 0 changed files. Plain 4995 with 1 skipped and Native 1153 with 2 skipped passed. Lost crossings were 0, and the log ends \"gate passed\".\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 4 checked, 0 flagged. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound apart from the finding. A build-then-commit plan with a keyed match is the right shape for keeping a press alive.\n\nSimplification: `cardIdentity()` now serves both the section state and the gesture check. That is good. The key-gesture state machine in reconcile.ts lines 33 to 103 is dense. A short comment per state (active, released, cancelled, completed) would help the next reader, but I do not require it.\n\nThe note's two limits stand: a press during a background read, and LW-1. This verdict authorizes no push, landing or deployment.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5653ae0d0b110e0e4e6252f5b48674fb046922eb","binding":"assigned","head":"cd086606e2ce01cb1efbfa3f671b1428fe2c5784","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c2d8971233af20e34b2c069bddf7590be00fa42f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:340635c1acf9bc9cb3657de587cd5a8679d24934\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c2d8971233af20e34b2c069bddf7590be00fa42f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}